Because borrowed capital is not just capital. It is capital plus a fixed claim on future cash. A young business usually has uncertain revenue, while debt repayment can be rigid. The danger is the mismatch: $\hbox{debt pressure}
=
\hbox{fixed repayment}
-
\hbox{reliable surplus cash flow}.$ When reliable surplus cash flow is small or irregular, the repayment schedule starts to govern the company. The warning is therefore not anti-growth. It is a warning about growing under an obligation that may not respect the timing of the business.

